The first candidate that was turned away

The beacon says which check of the passive match said no, and what it wanted
against what the nub is called.  It does not say which candidate said it:
the class after the beacon is the last one turned away, and the catalogue
offers the highest score first, so that is the donor's Apple personality
rather than ours.

So the class of the first one turned away is printed too, after an f.  The
first one is ours: our platform personality carries the highest score of
the lot.
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/startup.c b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
index 6ade0e8..a692180 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -528,6 +528,8 @@ char nio_cand_class[32] = "?";
 volatile unsigned int nio_match_fail = 0;
 char nio_match_want[24] = "-";
 char nio_match_have[24] = "-";
+char nio_first_class[24] = "-";
+volatile unsigned int nio_drop_count = 0;
 
 /* nio: a beacon that never sleeps.  The heartbeat thread below uses delay(),
  * which needs working timers; it prints HB 1 and stops, and that tells us
@@ -571,6 +573,8 @@ nio_beacon_thread(void *arg, wait_result_t wr)
 					nio_dc_puts("|");
 					nio_dc_puts(nio_match_have);
 				}
+				nio_dc_puts(" f");
+				nio_dc_puts(nio_first_class);
 				nio_dc_puts(" ");
 			}
 		}
